// Help view (Phase 5): the PawScout knowledge base. Plain-language guide to
// every feature, written for shop owners (future subscription users) - no
// internal jargon, no em-dashes, UK English. Prose was drafted from per-area
// fact sheets grounded in the code and then fact-checked against it; every
// number here (thresholds, defaults, timings) mirrors a real constant, so
// UPDATE THE RELEVANT TOPIC when a default changes. Diagrams live in
// ./help-diagrams.ts. Topics are native <details> disclosures (no JS).
import { inboxFlowSvg, orderFlowSvg, pricingChainSvg, marketsSvg, helpDiagramCss } from './help-diagrams';

export const helpView = `  <section class="card">
    <h2>PawScout guide</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="margin:0">Everything the console does, explained in plain language. Click any topic to expand it. Every number quoted here (thresholds, timings, defaults) is the shop's real behaviour, not an approximation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Getting started</h2>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>What PawScout does</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>PawScout is where you run every side of your pet store from one place. It curates and imports products from your supplier, CJDropshipping, so your catalogue stays full of items that are genuinely available. It keeps prices and stock honest by checking your supplier regularly, rather than showing numbers that go stale. When customers email you, the AI drafts a reply for you to read and approve, so you are never starting from a blank page. Once an order is paid, PawScout carries it through to fulfilment with your supplier. From what you sell, to what you say to customers, to how orders get dispatched, everything is visible and manageable from this one console.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Finding your way around</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The left-hand navigation has ten views: Glance, Inbox, Catalog, Operations, Audit, Partners, Campaigns, Traffic, Settings and Help. Glance gives you a snapshot of the whole shop. Inbox is where customer conversations and the AI's draft replies live. Catalog is where you manage the products you curate and import. Operations is where your orders sit as they move toward dispatch. Audit is the read-only record of every email sent and received. Partners tracks the discount vouchers you issue to collaborators. Campaigns is where you send announcements and offers to people who opted in. Traffic is your acquisition strategy board: every channel that could bring visitors, its state, and the next action. Settings holds your preferences, and Help is where guidance like this lives.</p><p>You can jump straight to any view by pressing the number keys: 1 to 8 for Glance through Traffic, 9 for Settings and 0 for Help. Inside the Inbox, pressing j and k moves you down and up through the list of conversations, so you can work through them without reaching for your mouse. The Inbox item in the navigation also carries an orange badge showing the number of drafts waiting for you. As you clear them the number falls, and once there are none left the badge disappears.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Shop at a Glance</h2>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Reading the Glance screen</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Glance is the first screen you see when you open PawScout, and pressing Refresh brings it fully up to date. Across the top sits a row of tiles, and every number on them is a real count, never an estimate.</p><ul><li><strong>Inbox to process</strong> shows how many conversations are waiting for you - always exactly the number of threads you will find when you open the Inbox, and the same number the Inbox button's badge shows. The small print underneath tells you how many of them already have an AI-written draft ready, with the age of the oldest; a thread whose draft is still being prepared counts too.</li><li><strong>Auto-sent in the last 24 hours</strong> shows replies PawScout has sent on its own.</li><li><strong>Orders held</strong> shows orders that need a decision from you.</li><li><strong>Orders in pipeline</strong> shows paid orders moving toward dispatch, with a breakdown of where each one sits.</li><li><strong>Live products</strong> shows how many imported products are currently selling.</li><li><strong>Low stock</strong> shows products down to 10 or fewer at your supplier, rechecked every 15 minutes.</li></ul><p>Together these tiles tell you, in one look, whether the shop needs anything from you right now.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>The attention queue</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Below the tiles sits the "Needs your attention" list. It shows only real items, and it puts the most urgent one at the top. You might see an order that is being held, with the reason it is stuck, a supplier order that is waiting for you to pay it, a draft reply that has been sitting unanswered, or a product that is close to selling out.</p><p>Each row carries a button that takes you straight to the view where you can deal with it, so you never have to hunt for the right screen yourself. If there is genuinely nothing that needs a decision, the list simply says "All clear", so you know at once that you can move on to other things rather than searching for a problem that is not actually there.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Activity and system health</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Further down Glance, Activity shows what has just happened in your shop: replies that went out, marked "auto" when PawScout sent them itself rather than you, emails that arrived, and orders that came in. It is a running record of real events, so you can see the order things happened in rather than a rounded-up summary.</p><p>System health tells you whether everything running behind the scenes is actually working. It shows when the nightly maintenance last completed, which runs at around 04:17, when the 15-minute order pipeline last completed, whether the connection to your supplier is working, and when mail last came in and went out. If any of these has no record to show, PawScout says "no record yet" rather than inventing a status, so you can always trust what you are looking at.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Market availability at a glance</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Your store sells to shoppers in several places, and not every product can reach every one of them. The market availability panel on Glance shows, for each international market, the European Union, Australia, and the US and Canada, how many of your products are currently excluded from view there.</p><p>These counts are reconciled every night, so they reflect what your supplier can actually deliver at that moment, rather than a fixed list you set once and forget. The point is to protect your shoppers abroad: if a product cannot realistically be delivered to someone in one of these markets, PawScout keeps it out of their view there, rather than letting them order something that cannot actually be delivered to them. Checking this panel tells you, at a glance, how tightly each market is currently being filtered.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Inbox and customer replies</h2>
    ${inboxFlowSvg}
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>How email becomes a drafted reply</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Whenever a customer writes to your shop's support address, that message flows into your Inbox automatically.</p><p>Every 15 minutes PawScout checks for new mail. It reads each conversation and sorts it into one of eight lanes, Shipping and Returns, Order Status, Product Questions, Sales Leads, Wholesale / Trade, Affiliate / Partnership, Complaints, or General. Each lane has its own specialist agent, and that agent writes a draft reply.</p><p>The draft then waits for you in the Inbox. Nothing is sent on your behalf unless you have chosen to allow it for that lane, so you always stay in control of what customers actually receive.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>The three-pane Inbox</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The Inbox is laid out as three panes so you can triage quickly without losing context.</p><p>On the left, the conversation list shows the customer's name when it is known, how long ago they wrote, which lane the conversation sits in, and an amber "draft ready" chip whenever a reply is waiting for you.</p><p>The middle pane is the conversation timeline, the full back and forth with that customer, ending with the latest draft if one exists.</p><p>The right pane is a details rail covering the customer's name and email, how many messages they have sent you, any labels on the conversation, why a draft is being held if it is on hold, partnership screening results where relevant, and the invoice builder for sending a payment request.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Reviewing and sending a draft</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>When PawScout has written a reply, it appears at the end of the timeline in a dashed orange card marked "DRAFT - not sent", along with chips showing the AI's confidence score, when it was drafted, the department, and any reasons it is on hold.</p><p>You can edit the draft directly in place. Click into the text, change whatever you like, then press Approve and send. Whatever is sitting in the box at that moment is exactly what the customer receives, so read it over before sending.</p><p>If a draft is not right, Regenerate throws it away and writes a fresh one. It never sends anything and never closes the conversation, so it is completely safe to use.</p><p>Every reply carries the right sign off automatically, so you never need to add one yourself. Use j and k to move between conversations, and if you have unsent edits, PawScout will ask you to confirm before switching away, so nothing is ever lost silently.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Replacing a draft with a template</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Sometimes you know exactly which reply a conversation needs - the partnership screening questions, a decline, a short holding note - but the drafted reply is something else. The "Use a template" dropdown next to the draft buttons swaps the draft for the template you pick, greeted with the sender's name, with nothing to paste in from anywhere. It also appears on conversations with no draft at all, where picking a template creates one.</p><p>Templates never send anything: the result is an ordinary editable draft, and the reply only goes out when you press Approve and send. Picking one replaces whatever is in the draft box, so apply the template first and make your edits after.</p><p>The templates do the same bookkeeping the automatic screening would: the screening questions put the sender into the partnership funnel, so their answers come back to it and are scored like any other applicant's, and if their message negotiated commission against promised sales the earned-tiers terms are included automatically. The declines record the decision so the funnel will not pitch them again, and the trial invite marks them as passed - issue their actual code from the Partners view.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Why a draft is on hold</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Sometimes a draft is written but not sent automatically, even when automatic replies are switched on. When that happens, a chip on the draft tells you why.</p><p>Common reasons are that reply mode is switched off for that particular lane, that the AI's confidence in the answer fell below your threshold, that the draft mentions a price or any money and so needs your eyes first, or that this customer has already received three automatic replies in the last 24 hours.</p><p>None of these holds mean anything has gone wrong. They simply mean PawScout would rather you had a quick look before the reply goes out. Read the draft, edit it if needed, and press Approve and send when you are happy.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Manual vs automatic replies</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Reply mode starts out as Manual for every lane, which means nothing is ever emailed to a customer without you clicking Approve and send.</p><p>Automatic mode is available if you want PawScout to send some replies on its own, but you stay in control of exactly which lanes are allowed to do this. There is a master switch and a separate switch for each lane, and both need to be on before that lane can send automatically.</p><p>Even then, a reply only goes out on its own once every one of these passes: the master switch is on, that lane's switch is on, the AI's confidence is at or above your threshold (80 out of 100 by default), the reply mentions no prices or money, and the customer has had fewer than three automatic replies in the last 24 hours. The Inbox header always shows a read only pill with the current mode, and you change the switches in Settings.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Conversation statuses and filters</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Every conversation moves through a simple lifecycle: New, Needs reply, Awaiting customer, Converted, Closed, or Spam.</p><p>When you send a reply, whether written by you or approved from a draft, the conversation moves to Awaiting customer. That means it drops off the Open filter, so your list only ever shows conversations that genuinely need your attention.</p><p>The Inbox gives you three quick filters, Open, which is unprocessed conversations and is the default view, Needs reply, and All, plus a full status dropdown if you want to look at a specific stage such as Converted or Spam. This makes it easy to work through what is outstanding without wading through everything you have already handled.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Sending an invoice from a conversation</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>If a conversation turns into a sale, you can invoice the customer without leaving the Inbox. The invoice builder sits in the details rail on the right of the conversation.</p><p>Search your catalogue, add the items and quantities the customer wants, and optionally write a short cover note. Then press Approve and send invoice. PawScout creates a Shopify draft order behind the scenes and emails the customer a secure Shopify payment link.</p><p>Any open invoice shows on the conversation, with a Discard option if you need to cancel it. Once the customer pays, the conversation is automatically marked Converted, so you can see at a glance which enquiries have turned into orders.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Catalog and pricing</h2>
    ${pricingChainSvg}
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>How products get into your store</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Every product that reaches your storefront has passed through several checks first. PawScout starts with the <strong>quality gates</strong>, simple rules around popularity, cost and how well a product matches what shoppers are searching for. Products that clear those gates then go through <strong>AI curation</strong>, which scores each one for quality and fit and only lets the strongest candidates through. The AI also rewrites the title and description into clean, ready to sell shop copy before anything goes live.</p><p>Once a product is approved, the <strong>collection map</strong> decides where it lands. Each storefront collection, such as pet toys, is tied to a matching tag from your supplier, so new products file themselves into the right place automatically. You never have to sort new arrivals by hand.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Quality gates</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Quality gates are the mechanical rules PawScout checks before a product is even considered for your store. Every candidate must clear all of these:</p><ul><li><strong>Popularity floor</strong> (default 1): how often the supplier item is listed by other sellers.</li><li><strong>Supplier cost band</strong> (default 5 to 80 US dollars): keeps products within a sensible price range from your supplier.</li><li><strong>Relevance rule</strong> (default Title): Loose accepts any keyword match, Title requires the keyword to appear in the product title, and Strict requires every keyword to appear in the title.</li><li><strong>Target per collection</strong> (default 12): how many products PawScout aims to keep stocked in each collection.</li></ul><p>You can adjust any of these to loosen or tighten what gets through.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>AI curation</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Once a product clears the quality gates, the AI takes a closer look. It scores every candidate for overall quality and how well it fits your store, and only admits products that meet or beat your <strong>minimum admission score</strong>, which defaults to 60 out of 100. Raise the score to be more selective, or lower it to let more products through.</p><p>Alongside scoring, the AI rewrites titles and descriptions into clean, consistent shop copy, so you are not left with rough supplier wording on your storefront. If you would rather skip this step entirely, you can switch curation off, leaving only the mechanical quality gates to decide what gets in. The AI models used for curation are chosen in Settings.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>How prices are set</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>PawScout turns your supplier's landed cost, the product price plus freight in US dollars, into your retail price in pounds using one formula:</p><ul><li>Convert the cost to pounds at your exchange rate (default 0.78), plus a safety buffer (default 2 percent) to absorb day to day currency movements.</li><li>Multiply the result up (default 2.5 times, with a floor of 2.2 times) so your margin never gets squeezed too thin.</li><li>Enforce a minimum margin (default 6 pounds), so even your cheapest products still earn you something worthwhile.</li><li>Optionally cap the margin at a maximum (off by default). With a cap set, expensive products stop climbing at cost plus the cap instead of the full multiplier, which keeps big-ticket items close to their market price. Leave the field blank to disable it.</li></ul><p>The default multiplier is designed to leave you with roughly 17 percent net profit once advertising, fees and refunds are accounted for, not just a healthy looking markup on paper.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Add-on products</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Add-on products are a small, hidden pool of cheap items, by default costing between 0.50 and 5 US dollars from your supplier, with a pool size of 12. Shoppers never come across them while browsing or searching your storefront. Instead, they appear only in two places: as <em>Complete the kit</em> suggestions on product pages, and as part of the free delivery nudge in the cart, encouraging shoppers to add just a little more to their basket.</p><p>You are in full control of this lane. You can make these products visible in the storefront like any other item, change the cost band or pool size to suit your store, or switch the whole lane off if you would rather not use it.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Search demand and supplier backfill</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Search demand shows you the last 30 days of what shoppers have typed into your storefront search, alongside how full each collection is compared with its target floor. This gives you a clear picture of where demand is going unmet, so you know which gaps are worth filling.</p><p>When a shopper searches for something your store does not currently stock, PawScout quietly checks your supplier's wider range behind the scenes and can show matching products drawn from there. If the shopper opens one of these, PawScout imports it in the background there and then, so a proper product page exists moments later, ready for the next shopper who comes looking for the same thing.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Automatic catalog upkeep</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Each night, PawScout runs a set of maintenance jobs to keep your catalogue in good shape:</p><ul><li>Prices and stock levels are re-synced with your supplier.</li><li>Collections that fall below their floor of 8 products are topped up from the vetted pool.</li><li>Product images and descriptions are cleaned up.</li><li>Any product whose title clearly belongs in a different collection gets re-filed there.</li></ul><p>Products that attract no views and no orders for 30 days are automatically archived to keep your catalogue fresh, though every new product gets 30 days of grace before this rule can touch it. A <em>Selling fast</em> badge appears on a product once it has had at least 3 real orders in the past 7 days, and quietly disappears again once demand cools off. It is never faked or applied by guesswork.</p><p>If you do not want to wait for the nightly run, the Catalog view gives you run-now buttons for each of these jobs too: hero image review, description rewriting, collection re-filing, and a full price and stock sync.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Operations and orders</h2>
    ${orderFlowSvg}
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>What happens after a customer pays</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The moment a customer pays, their order enters PawScout and is checked immediately for anything that needs your attention. Most orders pass every check and are simply queued for sending to your supplier. A small number are held instead, waiting for you to look at them first.</p><p>Queued orders do not go anywhere straight away. They sit in a sixty minute grace window first, so a customer has time to email you about a change of mind or a wrong address, and so you have time to step in if something looks off.</p><p>Every fifteen minutes, PawScout submits any orders that are due and checks your supplier for tracking numbers on orders already placed. As soon as a tracking number appears, it is written back to Shopify, the order is marked fulfilled, and your customer receives their tracking automatically.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Why an order is held</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>An order is held for your review rather than sent straight to your supplier whenever something needs a human eye on it first. This happens if an item in the order cannot be matched to a supplier product, if the delivery address is incomplete, if Shopify flags the order as a high fraud risk, if the order total is over one hundred pounds, or if payment has not been fully captured yet.</p><p>Orders can also be held because a product has sold out at the supplier, which has its own topic below.</p><p>Held orders show two action buttons, <strong>Release</strong>, which sends the order on its way, and <strong>Cancel</strong>; queued orders show <strong>Cancel</strong>. The orders table also shows you each order's total, its status, the reason for any hold, the supplier order number once one exists, and tracking as soon as it is available.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Fulfilment modes</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>In Operations you choose how PawScout handles fulfilment, and you can change this at any time.</p><p><strong>Manual</strong> is the default. Orders queue up and nothing is sent to your supplier until you act.</p><p><strong>CJ app</strong> hands fulfilment over to CJDropshipping's own Shopify app, which manages the order itself while PawScout stays out of the way.</p><p><strong>PawScout auto</strong> is the only mode that spends money on your behalf. PawScout creates the order with your supplier and pays for it from your supplier wallet, then tracks it through to delivery. Because it spends money automatically, switching to it asks you to confirm twice. You will also need to fund your supplier wallet first and switch off your supplier's own order import, so the same order is never placed twice.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Create-only and payment alerts</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p><strong>Create-only</strong> is a variant of PawScout auto. PawScout still creates the order with your supplier automatically, but it leaves the payment for you to make yourself on your supplier's site, rather than paying from your wallet.</p><p>Any order sitting unpaid shows an <strong>awaiting payment</strong> banner in Operations, so you can see at a glance which orders still need you to pay. You can also switch on an email alert so you are notified as each one is created, rather than having to check Operations yourself.</p><p>The alert email field is where you set which address these notifications go to. Use <strong>Send test email</strong> to check the connection is working before you rely on it.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Sold out at the supplier</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Before PawScout creates any order with your supplier, it checks live stock for every item in it. This catches products that have sold out at the supplier since the customer placed their order.</p><p>If something is out of stock, PawScout holds the order with a stockout reason and immediately zeroes that product's stock in your shop, so no other customer can buy something you cannot get hold of.</p><p>You then get an email with a recommended playbook to work through, check when the product is due to restock, offer the customer the choice to wait, or refund them or suggest an alternative. You stay in control of the decision, PawScout just makes sure nobody else can order the same item in the meantime.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>The shipping quote tool</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The shipping quote tool lets you check your supplier's live delivery options for a single product, to any destination country, for any quantity, without creating a real order.</p><p>For each available delivery service it shows you the price in US dollars, the same price converted to pounds, and the delivery time range in days. It reflects exactly what a real order would cost and how long it would take, so you can answer a customer's shipping question or check a market before you commit to anything.</p><p>Operations also includes a read only connection check for the ordering system itself, so you can confirm PawScout is talking to your supplier correctly at any time.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Sync now</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The <strong>Sync now</strong> button lets you run PawScout's nightly price and stock refresh on demand, rather than waiting for it to happen overnight.</p><p>When you run it, PawScout refreshes prices and stock across your catalogue, then tops up any collection that has fallen below its floor by pulling in more products from your vetted product pool. You can watch its progress as it works, so you know it is running rather than wondering whether anything happened.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Shipping, markets and stock safety</h2>
    ${marketsSvg}
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>How delivery estimates are made</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Your supplier holds stock in warehouses around the world, for example in Britain, the United States and China. How long a delivery takes depends entirely on which warehouse holds that particular product and where your customer lives, so there is no single answer for every order.</p><p>PawScout works this out for you every time. For every delivery estimate, whether it is in a customer email or shown on your storefront, it only considers warehouses that <strong>actually serve that customer's market</strong>, the same rule that decides which products each market sells, and uses the nearest of those with the item in stock. You get a straightforward, honest day range built from your supplier's real delivery data, not a guess.</p><p>The same rule keeps email replies honest at the edges: if a customer asks about an item their country's store does not carry, or writes from a country you do not deliver to, the reply says so plainly instead of quoting a courier route that checkout would refuse.</p><p>PawScout never tells a customer which warehouse or country their order is coming from. It simply gives them the timing they need to know, so your customers get accurate expectations without you having to explain sourcing details yourself.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Free UK delivery on British-stocked items</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Any product held in the supplier's British warehouse ships free to UK customers at checkout. You do not need to manage this yourself, PawScout keeps the list of British-stocked products up to date every night, so as items move in and out of that warehouse, which ones qualify for free UK delivery updates automatically.</p><p>In Operations, the <strong>Shipping profiles</strong> panel shows you the current count of products qualifying for free UK delivery, along with a button to sync the list immediately if you do not want to wait for the nightly update.</p><p>There is also a separate switch that controls whether your email assistant is allowed to <em>say</em> "free UK delivery" to a customer in a reply. It stays off until a real UK checkout has actually been verified at zero pounds, so you never claim something before it is proven. Checkout itself always charges free UK delivery on qualifying products regardless of this switch, it only controls the wording used in replies.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Selling in four markets, each from its own local stock</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Your store sells into four markets: the United Kingdom as your primary market, the European Union, the United States and Canada, and Australia. Each market is built around one idea: local buyers are shown products held in warehouses local to them, because that is the only way to promise both fast delivery and fair shipping cost. British-stocked items belong to the UK market, American stock to the US and Canada, and so on. A product stocked only in an American warehouse never appears to a British shopper, whatever the courier routes might allow.</p><p>While each market's local range is still growing, products from the supplier's China warehouse fill the remaining shelf space, but only where they can genuinely be delivered. As a market's local range matures it can be switched to local-only, and the China-stocked items quietly drop out of that market.</p><p>PawScout re-checks every product against every market every fifteen minutes, so a newly published product lands only in the market it belongs to, and anything that stops belonging is removed within minutes, checkout included. The <strong>Glance</strong> screen shows the current number of products excluded from each market.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Honest stock numbers</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>PawScout refreshes your stock figures from your supplier every night, so the numbers behind the scenes stay close to reality. When a product's supplier stock drops to 10 units or fewer, PawScout switches into closer watch on that item, checking it every 15 minutes instead of waiting for the next nightly refresh.</p><p>At that point, PawScout also keeps <strong>one unit back in reserve</strong>, selling one fewer than the supplier actually reports, and the very last unit is never offered for sale. This protects you from two shops selling the same final item at the same moment.</p><ul><li>10 or fewer: the storefront shows the real number, for example "Only 6 left in stock".</li><li>3 or fewer: that number gets "Almost gone" styling to catch attention.</li><li>Above 10: customers simply see "In stock", with no number shown.</li></ul><p>Purchase limits on the product page and in the cart follow these same honest numbers, so nobody can order more than can genuinely be supplied.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>When stock runs out anyway</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Even with nightly refreshes and the 15-minute low-stock watch, timing means a product can still sell out at the exact wrong moment, for instance if the very last unit goes elsewhere seconds before your customer's order comes in.</p><p>When that happens, PawScout does not let the order fail silently. It holds the order rather than losing it, zeroes the stock in your shop immediately so no one else can order the same item, and emails you straight away with next steps so you can put things right with the customer.</p><p>For more on handling that email and what to do next, see the Operations help.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Settings and automation</h2>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Connecting your supplier</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>You connect PawScout to your CJDropshipping account by pasting in your account's access key once. PawScout checks that the key works before it saves anything, so you know straight away if something is wrong. Once saved, the key is stored <strong>encrypted</strong>, it is never shown back to you in plain text.</p><p>This one connection is what powers everything supplier-related: searching for products, checking stock, pulling prices, getting delivery quotes and creating orders with your supplier. If you ever want to check that the connection is still working, look at the <strong>Glance</strong> health panel, it shows you at a glance whether your supplier connection is healthy or needs attention.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Choosing AI models</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>PawScout gives you three dropdowns to choose the AI models behind your shop. The <strong>image model</strong> reviews product photos, for example choosing the best main image, so only image-capable models appear in that list. The <strong>text model</strong> powers everything written, including inbox reply drafting, product curation scoring and copy rewriting, so it is a shop-wide choice that affects your whole store, not just one feature. The <strong>fallback model</strong> steps in if the image model fails.</p><p>Each dropdown shows a live price for every model on the list, covering the text it reads and writes, so you can see the relative cost before you pick one. Leaving a dropdown set to <em>top model</em> lets PawScout automatically use the best model available, a safe default if you are not sure. There is also a system-managed last resort behind the scenes, it steps in only if the main service is unavailable, so your shop keeps working.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Reply mode and per-lane switches</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The master switch in your reply settings chooses between <strong>Manual review</strong>, the default, where every reply waits for your approval before it sends, and <strong>Automatic</strong>. Switching to Automatic asks you to confirm first, because it means PawScout will email customers on its own whenever its checks pass, without you seeing the message beforehand.</p><p>Below the master switch, each of your eight inbox lanes has its own switch, and all of them start switched off. This lets you automate exactly one lane, for example automatically declining affiliate approaches, while every other lane stays on manual review. You can mix and match as you get comfortable.</p><p>The idea behind every switch in PawScout is the same: automation is opt-in, it is bounded by thresholds you control, and anything PawScout is not confident about waits for you.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Inbox thresholds and guidance</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Your inbox settings give you a few dials to control how PawScout behaves. The <strong>auto-confidence threshold</strong>, 80 out of 100 by default, is the minimum confidence a draft reply needs before automatic sending is even considered for it. The <strong>intent threshold</strong>, 70 by default, controls how strongly a message has to look like a genuine sales lead before lead handling treats it as one.</p><p>The <strong>alert email</strong> is where PawScout sends notifications that need your personal attention, so make sure it is an address you check regularly.</p><p><strong>Reply guidance</strong> is a free-text box where you write standing instructions, in your own voice, that every draft follows, things like tone or phrases you want used or avoided. A department signature is always added automatically to every reply, so do not put sign-offs in your guidance, just the instructions themselves.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Automatic invoicing limits</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Auto-invoice is switched off by default. When you turn it on, PawScout can create and send an invoice to a customer without you clicking anything, but only when three bounds are all met at once.</p><ul><li>PawScout's confidence in what the customer wants must be at or above the invoice confidence threshold, <strong>95 out of 100</strong> by default.</li><li>The invoice total must be at or below the value ceiling, <strong>250 pounds</strong> by default.</li><li>That customer must not have already received more than the rate limit of invoices in the last 24 hours, <strong>2</strong> by default.</li></ul><p>If an invoice falls outside any one of those bounds, PawScout does not send it automatically, it waits for you to review and send it yourself. You can adjust each of the three numbers to make automatic invoicing stricter or more relaxed.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Reading your current configuration</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Current configuration is a read-only summary that shows you everything as PawScout sees it right now, in one place. It covers your supplier connection, the gates and thresholds you have set, your price range, target counts, your pricing rule, the add-on lane and how your collections are mapped.</p><p>You cannot change anything from this screen, it is a snapshot rather than a settings form. It is most useful when you want to check your setup at a glance without hunting through every settings page, or when you are talking to support and need to describe exactly how your shop is configured right now.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>The System states register</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>System states is the definitive answer to "what is the system actually enforcing right now?". It lists every switch and setting across the whole of PawScout in one table: reply automation and each lane's auto-send, invoicing bounds, partnership handling, email tracking and the postal address, fulfilment mode and the delivery-time envelope, catalog gates and pricing, the supplier and email connections, and when each background job last completed.</p><p>The crucial property is that nothing on the table is stored. Every value is read at the moment the page loads, from the exact same place the running code reads when it enforces that setting, and the email tracking row goes further by checking the evidence: the sending domain's measurement flags, the webhook secret, and whether events have actually arrived. A note, a memory or a to-do list can go stale the moment you act; this table cannot. When in doubt about any toggle, trust this table over anything anyone remembers, including us.</p><p>Green means active, red means off or missing, and plain values are informational settings rather than switches. The small grey text under each entry names where the value lives, and Refresh re-reads everything on demand.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Audit log</h2>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Reviewing every email sent and received</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The Audit view lists everyone who has emailed your shop, most recent contact first, showing their name where known, their email address, how many messages you have exchanged and which categories their conversations fall under. Use the category filter to narrow the list, for example to only sales enquiries or only affiliate approaches.</p><p>Click a person to open the whole conversation in one scrollable window, newest message first, across every thread you have had with them. Each sent message shows exactly how it went out: <strong>sent automatically</strong> means PawScout sent it on its own under your reply settings, <strong>approved by you</strong> means you reviewed the draft and approved it before it went, and <strong>system message</strong> covers automatic mail with no reviewed draft behind it, such as an invoice email.</p><p>Nothing in this view can send or change anything, it exists purely so you can check what was said, to whom, and on whose authority.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Partners and vouchers</h2>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Running an affiliate voucher trial</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The Partners view is where you test a collaborator before committing to a longer partnership. You give them their own discount code with an end date, they share it with their audience, and every order placed with that code is tracked here automatically. Because each partner has a unique code, you always know exactly which sales they drove, and if a code ever escapes to a coupon site you can revoke that one code without touching anyone else's.</p><p>The fastest way to start a trial is <strong>Issue automatically</strong>: PawScout creates the discount code in Shopify for you, set up safely (a percentage off, once per customer, no combining with other discounts, a usage cap and an end date), registers it for tracking, and, when the partner already has a conversation in your Inbox, stages the invitation email carrying the code for your approval. Codes are timed around your promotions: if a storewide event is running, the new code is scheduled to start right after the event ends, so a partner code never competes with a bigger discount. A banner above the form shows what is currently running and how new codes will be timed. The manual path still exists if you prefer to create the code in Shopify admin yourself and register it here.</p><p>From the moment a code exists, PawScout checks for new and updated orders about every 15 minutes and keeps the numbers current.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>The trial code queue (qualify now, issue later)</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>When you are working through partnership threads in the Inbox, you often decide someone deserves a trial before you are ready to hand out codes, for example while a storewide event is running. Open their thread and press <strong>Add to trial code queue</strong> in the Partnership screening panel on the right. That parks them here in Partners under "Waiting for a code": nothing is sent and no code is created, so you can process the whole mailbox in one sitting without losing track of who qualified.</p><p>When you are ready, each queued row has <strong>Issue code now</strong>: one click (confirmed with a second) creates their code in Shopify on the standard terms - 5% off for their audience, 2% commission, a 30-day window - with the usual promotion-aware timing, and stages the invitation email carrying the code back on their inbox thread for your approval. Prefer different terms for someone? Use the issue form below with their email instead. Remove takes someone out of the queue without issuing; you can always queue them again from their thread.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>How partnership pitches are screened</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>When someone writes in offering to promote the shop, PawScout drafts a short set of qualifying questions for you to approve: where they would feature you, their audience and engagement, how and when they would post, past brand work, and whether the trial terms suit them. When they answer, the trial itself is the test: claims are neither believed nor argued with, because a tracked discount code measures delivered sales directly. Only genuine abuse ends the conversation, coupon or deal-site channels, plans to bid on the brand name, prohibited tactics, or refusing the basic terms; everyone else passes to a voucher trial, and their answers are scored purely for your context. Screening answers can never be re-filed by the spam classifier: once someone is in the funnel, their replies always come back to it. By default every message still waits for your approval; if you switch on <strong>Auto-issue voucher trials</strong> in Settings (and the Reply mode master is on Automatic), a passing candidate's code is created and their invitation sent without you, and you are alerted with the code so you can watch it in Partners.</p><p>Two pitch patterns get special handling, and they are caught wherever they land: these senders usually write to the general contact address, often opening with an innocent question before the real pitch arrives in a follow-up, so PawScout judges the newest message's own words rather than relying on the mailbox or the original classification. Someone negotiating commission against promised sales volume ("give me X% and I will sell Y units") receives the same qualifying questions plus a clear statement of the earned ladder: everyone starts at the standard 2% of face value, the first tier review comes after 25 tracked orders in a 30-day window, and consistent performers can reach 5%. Because every order is tracked in the ledger, their delivered results do the negotiating, never their promises. And a pure service pitch, an agency offering site speed work, SEO or listing services, is not a partnership at all: it gets a polite, final decline explaining that development, listings and marketing stay in-house. If a message shows any sign of being a real creator, it always gets the funnel rather than the decline.</p><p>You choose how pitches are handled in Settings under "Partnership approaches": screen them for a voucher trial (the default), screen them for the future link-based programme, or politely decline every approach, which was the previous policy and remains available as a switch.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>How sales and commission are counted</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Gross is the face value of the goods: what they cost before the voucher discount, excluding shipping. Commission is your agreed percentage of that gross, and it only counts for orders that have actually been paid. If an order is refunded, the commission on it is removed automatically, and a partial refund reduces it proportionally. This face-value basis is deliberate: it is the number the partner can verify themselves, so there is never an argument about what "sales" means.</p><p>Each row shows the commission currently owed and what you have already paid out. When you settle up with a partner, press "Mark commission paid" and the outstanding orders are stamped as paid, so the owed figure starts again from zero. Payouts themselves happen outside PawScout, for example by PayPal; the console keeps the ledger honest.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Ending or revoking a trial</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>A voucher ends by itself on its end date, which does the awkward work for you: continuing a partnership is a deliberate choice, not a default. If you need to stop one early, for example because the code leaked to a coupon site, press Revoke on the row and confirm. That records the revocation and deactivates the code in Shopify in the same click, so it stops working at checkout immediately; in the rare case Shopify cannot be reached, the message tells you to disable it by hand under Discounts. Orders already placed stay in the ledger exactly as they happened.</p><p>To run another round with the same partner, press <strong>Reissue</strong> on their revoked or expired row and confirm the second click. It repeats the automatic issue with the same terms: a fresh code created in Shopify with the usual promotion-aware timing, a new ledger row, and the invitation email staged in your Inbox if they have a conversation there. The button only appears while the partner has no live code, so you can never accidentally give one person two working discounts.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Campaigns and marketing email</h2>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Who campaigns can email</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Campaigns send only to people who have positively said yes to marketing, and PawScout enforces this in the sending machinery itself rather than trusting a list.</p><p>The <strong>buyers</strong> audience is customers who ticked "email me with news and offers" at your checkout. PawScout reads that consent from Shopify at the moment you press send, never from a stored copy, so someone who unsubscribed through Shopify five minutes ago is already off the list. The <strong>partners</strong> audience is collaborators you have marked as opted in on the Partners page, and you should only mark them after they have themselves said yes, which is exactly what the invitation email asks them. Writing to us about a partnership is not consent to receive marketing.</p><p>On top of both audiences sits one shared unsubscribe list. Anyone who clicks unsubscribe in any PawScout email, a campaign or a reorder reminder, is suppressed from all future marketing email permanently. Emails about orders a customer places are never affected by any of this.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Composing and sending a campaign</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Write the subject and message in plain text, choose the audience, and save. A saved campaign is only a draft: nothing sends until you act, and no campaign is ever sent by the overnight jobs. The audience sizes shown at the top of the view are live counts, so you can see who a campaign would reach before you commit.</p><p>The safe habit is: <strong>send yourself a test first</strong>. The test is the exact email your recipients would get, including the sign-off, your business address and a working unsubscribe link, with only the subject marked as a test. When you are happy, press send and confirm the second click. Sending happens in small batches that continue automatically, with progress shown as it goes.</p><p>Every send is recorded per recipient, so a campaign can never email the same person twice: if sending is interrupted part-way, pressing send again simply resumes where it stopped. Unsubscribed addresses are counted as suppressed and skipped, and you will see that count in the result.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Controlling a campaign: edit, pause, stop, duplicate</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Each campaign row shows the controls that make sense for its state. A <strong>draft</strong> can be edited (the composer fills with its content and saves back to the same draft), tested, sent, or discarded. While a campaign is <strong>sending</strong> you can pause or stop it; a <strong>paused</strong> campaign can be resumed or stopped.</p><p><strong>Pause</strong> is instant and reversible: the small batch already in flight finishes (at most 25 emails), then nothing more goes out until you press Resume. Nobody is emailed twice on resume - the per-recipient ledger sees to that. <strong>Stop</strong> is final: the campaign is closed where it stands, the record of who already received it stays, and sending it again is deliberately impossible. Both Stop and Resume ask for a second click.</p><p>Emails already delivered cannot be recalled - that is how email works - so the controls are about the rest of the audience, and the test-first habit is about catching mistakes before any of this is needed. A finished campaign, sent or stopped, offers <strong>Duplicate as draft</strong>: a fresh, fully editable copy for the next round, with its own clean ledger.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Staying on the right side of email law</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Marketing email has legal rules, and PawScout is built so following them is automatic. Every campaign email identifies who it is from, includes your business postal address in the small print, and carries a one-click unsubscribe that works immediately. The postal address is a hard legal requirement for email to recipients in the United States and good practice everywhere, which is why Campaigns refuses to send until you have saved one under Sender details.</p><p>The dividing line to understand is correspondence versus marketing. Replying to someone who emailed you is correspondence and needs no consent, which is why the Inbox works the way it does. A broadcast you initiate is marketing and needs consent, which is why Campaigns is gated on the checkout opt-in for buyers and the recorded opt-in for partners.</p><p>Two things remain yours to keep in order outside PawScout: your store's privacy policy should mention that subscribers receive marketing email, that opens and clicks are measured, and how to opt out; and as a business processing personal data in the UK you are expected to be registered with the Information Commissioner's Office, which charges most small businesses a modest yearly fee.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Running storewide events (the Events view)</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The Events view is the store's promotion calendar. The strip at the top shows what is running or scheduled right now, read live from Shopify, with an End now button on the running event. Below it sit the <strong>yearly event cards</strong>: every UK retail moment pre-built with its design and wording - the bank holidays, Black Friday to Cyber Monday, the Boxing Day sale - and dated by calculation, so each card always shows its next occurrence's real dates without anyone looking them up.</p><p>Enabling a card is the whole job. The discount is scheduled in Shopify immediately with the dates inside it (the code only works during its window), the storefront pop-up wakes up when the event starts wearing that event's own headline, wording and search shortcuts, the announcement email is drafted for you about two weeks before each occurrence (you still review and send it), and when the event ends everything tears itself down. After that the card re-dates itself to next year and the cycle repeats - enable once, runs every year until you disable it. The pop-up headline always shows the live percentage, so changing an event's discount can never leave the storefront advertising the wrong number.</p><p>The <strong>one-off event</strong> form covers promotions outside the calendar, with the same machinery. Two rules keep the calendar honest: no two events can ever run at the same time, and bank-holiday windows are reserved for their own cards - a one-off event on those dates is refused with a pointer to the right card. Partner voucher codes automatically schedule themselves around every event, yearly or one-off.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Email analytics: opens, clicks, bounces</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Turn on tracking once from the Email analytics panel and every email sent from then on reports back what happened to it. Campaign rows show delivered, opened and clicked counts, each counting unique people rather than repeat events, so one enthusiastic reader opening five times still counts once. Test sends to yourself are excluded from the numbers.</p><p>Read the numbers with the right amount of trust: <strong>clicks are the reliable signal</strong>, because a click is a deliberate act. Opens are approximate in both directions, since some mail apps load emails invisibly (which inflates opens) and others block the tracking image (which hides real opens). Treat opens as a trend line between campaigns, not a headcount.</p><p>The quiet benefit is deliverability: an address whose mail server permanently rejects our email, or a recipient who marks a message as spam, is put on the suppression list automatically and never mailed again. That protects your sending reputation as the audience grows. Tracking also rewrites links to pass through a measurement redirect on their way to the destination; this is standard marketing-email practice and invisible in normal use.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Traffic and growth</h2>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>The Traffic strategy board</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>Traffic is where acquisition strategy lives instead of in your head. Each row is one channel that could bring visitors: a platform, a partnership type, a content play. A channel carries a status (idea, testing, active or paused), a priority from 1 (work on it now) to 5 (someday), the reason it fits this store, and most importantly a single next action. If a channel's next action is not obvious, that is usually the sign it is not ready to work on.</p><p>Click any row to open it in the editor: change its status as reality changes, rewrite the next action when the current one is done, and keep the notes honest about what happened, with numbers and dates rather than impressions. New channels are added from the card at the bottom; deleting one asks for a second click, like every other consequential button in the console. Everything on the board is yours to reshape - it is a working document, not a report.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>The starter strategy, channel by channel</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The board arrives pre-seeded with seven channels chosen from what your store actually runs, not from a generic marketing checklist. Three are priority 1. <strong>Google free product listings</strong> puts your whole catalog in the Google Shopping tab for nothing but feed setup, and pet supplies live on product search - it is the biggest untouched lever. <strong>Email campaigns</strong> are already built and active, with consent, compliance and engagement measurement handled; the work left is simply sending good campaigns. The <strong>partner program</strong> is already testing itself: every applicant gets a tracked voucher trial, so the funnel produces its own evidence about whether affiliates convert.</p><p>Two are priority 2: <strong>search engine optimisation</strong> on your collections and products, the cheapest compounding asset because the catalog already exists, and <strong>short-video social</strong>, because pet content is the most forgiving organic niche on TikTok and Reels. Two are deliberately held back: <strong>reorder reminders</strong> are built but dormant until there is enough order volume to remind, and <strong>paid ads</strong> are parked until a product proves its unit economics - spending on traffic before the margin maths works is the classic way small stores lose money.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Working a channel: idea to active</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The statuses describe evidence, not enthusiasm. An <strong>idea</strong> is anything you have not started. Move it to <strong>testing</strong> the day you do its first next action, and from then on the notes field is the experiment log: what you tried, when, and what the numbers said. A channel earns <strong>active</strong> only when it demonstrably brings visitors or orders - if you cannot point at the evidence, it is still testing. <strong>Paused</strong> is an honest and useful verdict, not a failure: it means you looked, the timing or economics were wrong, and you wrote down why, so future you does not repeat the experiment for free.</p><p>The single next action is the board's engine. Keeping it to one concrete step per channel forces prioritisation and makes progress visible: a healthy board has next actions that keep changing, a stale one has the same sentences for weeks. When a priority 1 channel's next action has not moved in a while, either do it, demote the channel, or pause it with a reason.</p></div></details>
    <details class="hlp-topic"><summary>Where the evidence lives</summary><div class="hlp-body"><p>The board holds strategy; the evidence for it lives in the views you already use. Campaign performance - delivered, opened, clicked per campaign - shows on the Campaigns view once tracking is on, and those numbers are the verdict on the email channel. Partner performance lives on the Partners view, where every voucher shows the orders attributed to its code: that is the verdict on the affiliate channel. For search and social you will be reading numbers outside PawScout (Google Search Console, the platforms' own analytics) until those are wired in; bring the conclusions back into the channel's notes so the board stays the one place where the whole picture lives.</p><p>When a strategy discussion produces a decision, record it on the channel it concerns: the status change, the new next action, a one-line note with the date. The board is only as good as its last honest update.</p></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
    <ul class="cols">
      <li><code>1</code> - <code>8</code> <span class="muted">switch view (Glance, Inbox, Catalog, Operations, Audit, Partners, Campaigns, Traffic)</span></li>
      <li><code>9</code> / <code>0</code> <span class="muted">Settings / Help</span></li>
      <li><code>j</code> / <code>k</code> <span class="muted">next / previous conversation in the Inbox list</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="sub muted" style="margin:10px 0 0">Shortcuts are ignored while you are typing, and none of them can send anything - approving a reply is always a click.</p>
  </section>`;

